  "worked_solution": "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Since the function equals zero at $x=-5$ and at $x=2,$ we include these endpoints in the solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in [\\,-5,-1\\,)\u22c3[\\,2,\\infty ).\n$$",
